  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` is now fast, and its output is unchanged. `npm test` passes (4 of 4). I didn't print the timing for 200,000 orders. The new test only checks that it takes under 1 second, and it passes.\n\nThe slowness came from three quadratic spots in the order loop:\n- **Duplicate check:** `seen.includes(order.id)` rescanned every id seen so far. It's now a `Set`.\n- **Customer lookup:** `groups.find(...)` scanned the group list for every order. It's now a `Map` keyed by customer id.\n- **Re-sorting:** the whole leaderboard was re-sorted after every order. It's now sorted once at the end, after filtering to customers with spend above 0.\n\nSorting once gives the same order as before. The ranking breaks ties by spend, then first order time, then customer id, and customer ids are unique, so no two customers tie fully. The filter, `limit` and `rank` handling are untouched.\n\nI added two tests in `test/report.test.js`:\n- **Equivalence test:** it keeps a copy of the original implementation and compares the old and new results with `deepStrictEqual`. The inputs are random orders with ties, duplicate ids, refunded and pending orders, EUR amounts and several limits.\n- **Speed test:** it runs 200,000 orders and fails if the call takes 1 second or more.\n\nI didn't try customer ids that are `NaN` or a mix of numbers and strings, which could behave slightly differently from the old lookup.",
